           OVERVIEW
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-part" id="overview" aria-labelledby="overview-h">
        <div class="tg-part__head">
          <span class="tg-part__kicker">Overview</span>
          <h2 class="tg-part__title" id="overview-h">Seven Claims, Not One</h2>
          <p class="tg-part__intro">Almost all bad argument on this subject — in both
          directions — comes from treating "the papacy" as a single package that must
          be swallowed or spat out whole. It is not one claim. It is a stack of seven,
          each of which needs its own evidence, and the stack is strongest at the
          bottom and weakest at the top.</p>
        </div>

        <section class="tg-section" id="unbundling">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Stack</h2>

          <p>Each step below is a separate historical or exegetical claim. Granting one
          does not grant the next. A Protestant who denies the first is arguing against
          the text; a Catholic who thinks the first proves the seventh has skipped five
          arguments.</p>

          <table class="pp-grade">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>#</th><th>The claim</th><th>Where it would have to come from</th><th>This study's verdict</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>1</td>
                <td>Peter held a real primacy among the Twelve.</td>
                <td>The Gospels and Acts.</td>
                <td class="pp-verdict">Strong</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>2</td>
                <td>Peter went to Rome and died there.</td>
                <td>Early historical witness.</td>
                <td class="pp-verdict">Strong</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>3</td>
                <td>Peter was "bishop of Rome" in the later monarchical sense.</td>
                <td>Second-century church order.</td>
                <td class="pp-verdict">Weak</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>4</td>
                <td>Peter's primacy was a transmissible <em>office</em>, not a personal distinction.</td>
                <td>Inference from <a class="ref" data-ref="Isaiah 22:22">Isaiah 22</a> and <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 1:20">Acts 1</a>.</td>
                <td class="pp-verdict">Contested inference</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>5</td>
                <td>That office passes specifically and <em>exclusively</em> to the bishop of Rome.</td>
                <td>Tradition; no scriptural statement exists.</td>
                <td class="pp-verdict">Tradition only</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>6</td>
                <td>The holder has universal, ordinary, immediate jurisdiction over every Christian.</td>
                <td>Vatican I, <em>Pastor Aeternus</em> ch. 3 (1870).</td>
                <td class="pp-verdict">Counter-evidenced</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>7</td>
                <td>He teaches infallibly under defined conditions.</td>
                <td>Vatican I, <em>Pastor Aeternus</em> ch. 4 (1870).</td>
                <td class="pp-verdict">Counter-evidenced</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <div class="tg-note">
            <span class="tg-note__label">Why the split matters pastorally</span>
            <p>A believer who has been told "Peter was just one apostle among equals"
            and then reads <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 16:18">Matthew 16</a> honestly
            can feel the floor move. That reaction is a symptom of having been handed a
            weak version of a position that has a strong version. The strong version
            grants claims 1 and 2 without flinching, and puts its weight exactly where
            the evidence thins — at 6 and 7. This study is built that way.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="what-1870-says">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">What Is Actually Being Claimed</h2>

          <p>It is worth reading the defined doctrine before arguing about it. Vatican I
          (1870) defined two things. First, jurisdiction:</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            We teach and declare that, by divine ordinance, the Roman church possesses a
            pre-eminence of ordinary power over every other church, and that this
            jurisdictional power of the Roman pontiff is both episcopal and immediate.
            Both clergy and faithful, of whatever rite and dignity, both singly and
            collectively, are bound to submit to this power by the duty of hierarchical
            subordination and true obedience.
            <cite>Vatican I, <em>Pastor Aeternus</em> ch. 3 (18 July 1870)</cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>Second, infallibility:</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            We teach and define as a divinely revealed dogma that when the Roman Pontiff
            speaks <em>ex cathedra</em>, that is, when, in the exercise of his office as
            shepherd and teacher of all Christians, in virtue of his supreme apostolic
            authority, he defines a doctrine concerning faith or morals to be held by the
            whole Church, he possesses, by the divine assistance promised to him in
            blessed Peter, that infallibility which the divine Redeemer willed his Church
            to enjoy in defining doctrine concerning faith or morals.
            <cite>Vatican I, <em>Pastor Aeternus</em> ch. 4 (18 July 1870)</cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>Three things follow immediately, and both sides should hold to them.</p>

          <div class="tg-point">
            <span class="tg-point__label">Impeccability is not claimed</span>
            <div class="tg-point__heading">Arguments that lead with the Borgias attack a
            claim no one makes.</div>
            <p>Nothing in the definition asserts that popes are holy, wise, or even
            personally orthodox in their private opinions. The tenth-century papacy and
            the Renaissance papacy are appalling by any standard, and Catholic historians
            say so more bluntly than Protestant ones — Eamon Duffy's <em>Saints and
            Sinners</em> is the standard example. But moral scandal is not evidence
            against the doctrine as defined, and time spent on it is time not spent on
            the actual question.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-point">
            <span class="tg-point__label">The conditions are narrow</span>
            <div class="tg-point__heading">Infallibility attaches to defining acts, not to
            everything a pope says.</div>
            <p>By common Catholic reckoning the definition has been invoked
            unambiguously twice — the Immaculate Conception (1854) and the Assumption
            (1950). Encyclicals, interviews, and off-hand remarks are not
            <em>ex cathedra</em>. Any critique that treats every papal sentence as a test
            case is critiquing a doctrine that does not exist.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-point">
            <span class="tg-point__label">But the definitions are strong</span>
            <div class="tg-point__heading">"Ordinary," "immediate," and "of whatever rite
            and dignity" are the load-bearing words.</div>
            <p><em>Ordinary</em> means the power belongs to the office rather than being
            delegated for a case; <em>immediate</em> means it reaches each Christian
            directly, not through their own bishop. That is a very large claim, and it is
            the one the historical record tests hardest. It is also the one the Christian
            East has always refused — while accepting apostolic succession, the
            episcopate, the sacraments, the councils, and tradition as authoritative.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="method">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">How This Study Handles Evidence</h2>

          <p>Four rules, stated up front so they can be checked.</p>

          <div class="tg-principle">
            <span class="tg-principle__label">1 · Primary sources, quoted, with the edition named</span>
            <p class="tg-principle__text">Every patristic and conciliar quotation in Part III
            is reproduced from a text you can open — most of them from this site's own
            <a href="../../library/">Library</a>. Where a text is quoted from a translation
            with known variants (Cyprian above all), the recension is named. The
            <a href="#sources">Sources</a> tab lists every one with its edition.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-principle">
            <span class="tg-principle__label">2 · Silence is weighed, not weaponised</span>
            <p class="tg-principle__text">"The New Testament never says X" is a real datum
            when X is the kind of thing the documents would be expected to say, and a weak
            one otherwise. The same standard is applied to both sides — including to
            arguments this study accepts.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-principle">
            <span class="tg-principle__label">3 · Each position is stated at full strength before it is answered</span>
            <p class="tg-principle__text">If you cannot state the other side's best argument
            in a form its advocates would sign, you do not yet know whether you disagree
            with it.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-principle">
            <span class="tg-principle__label">4 · The conclusion names its own prior</span>
            <p class="tg-principle__text">Part V reaches a verdict, and then says what has
            to be true about how God preserves his church for that verdict to hold, and
            what evidence would overturn it. A conclusion that cannot say what would
            change it is not a conclusion; it is a loyalty.</p>
          </div>
        </section>
      </section><!-- /overview -->

