      <section class="tg-part" id="part-1" aria-labelledby="part-1-h">
        <div class="tg-part__head">
          <span class="tg-part__kicker">Part I</span>
          <h2 class="tg-part__title" id="part-1-h">The Case For, at Its Strongest</h2>
          <p class="tg-part__intro">Everything in this part is the strongest form of the
          argument for Petrine primacy, stated without hedging and without an immediate
          rebuttal attached. Objections come in Part II. Reading them together is the
          only way to weigh them.</p>
          <p class="tg-part__verse"><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 16:19">Matthew 16:19</a> · <a class="ref" data-ref="Isaiah 22:22">Isaiah 22:22</a> · <a class="ref" data-ref="John 21:17">John 21:17</a> · <a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 22:32">Luke 22:32</a></p>
        </div>

        <nav class="tg-toc tg-toc--sticky" aria-label="Part I contents">
          <h2 class="tg-toc__title">In this part</h2>
          <ol class="tg-toc__list">
            <li><a href="#rock">The Rock and the Name</a></li>
            <li><a href="#keys">The Keys — and Why Isaiah 22 Is the Best Argument</a></li>
            <li><a href="#shepherd">Shepherd and Strengthener</a></li>
            <li><a href="#narrative">The Narrative Pattern</a></li>
            <li><a href="#for-history">The Early Historical Case</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <section class="tg-section" id="rock">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Rock and the Name</h2>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            Simon Peter answered, "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
            Jesus replied, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah! For this was not revealed
            to you by flesh and blood, but by My Father in heaven. And I tell you that you
            are Peter, and on this rock I will build My church, and the gates of Hades will
            not prevail against it. I will give you the keys of the kingdom of heaven.
            Whatever you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and whatever you loose on
            earth will be loosed in heaven."
            <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 16:16-19">Matthew 16:16–19</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>Three features of this text carry real weight, and one popular Protestant
          argument against it does not.</p>

          <h3 class="tg-subsection">The name is given, not observed</h3>

          <p>Jesus does not remark that Simon is rock-like. He renames him. In Scripture
          a divine renaming marks a change of vocation and standing —
          Abram to Abraham, Jacob to Israel. No other disciple is renamed by Jesus with a
          commission attached. The Sons of Thunder receive a nickname
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Mark 3:17">Mark 3:17</a>); Simon receives a
          designation that the church then uses as his name for the rest of the
          New Testament.</p>

          <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Aramaic collapses the wordplay gap</h3>

          <div class="tg-definition">
            <span class="tg-definition__term">Kepha</span>
            <span class="tg-definition__original">כֵּיפָא (Aramaic) · Κηφᾶς (Cephas) · Πέτρος / πέτρα (Petros / petra)</span>
            <span class="tg-definition__def">The Aramaic word underlying both halves of the
            saying is almost certainly the same: "you are <em>kepha</em>, and on this
            <em>kepha</em> I will build." Paul preserves the Aramaic form as Peter's actual
            name — <em>Cephas</em> — nine times
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 1:12">1 Cor 1:12</a>;
            <a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 2:9">Gal 2:9</a>, and elsewhere), and John
            gives the translation explicitly at
            <a class="ref" data-ref="John 1:42">John 1:42</a>. The Syriac Peshitta, in a
            language cognate with Aramaic, uses one word in both clauses.</span>
          </div>

          <p>Which is why the familiar Protestant argument — that <em>petros</em> means a
          small stone and <em>petra</em> a bedrock, so Peter is a pebble and the confession
          is the cliff — should be retired. The distinction is largely a feature of older
          poetic Greek and had substantially eroded by the first century. D. A. Carson,
          who is not arguing for the papacy, puts the grammatical point plainly: had
          Matthew wanted to say Peter was a mere stone in contrast to Christ the rock, the
          available word was <em>lithos</em>; the reason Greek shifts from
          <em>Petros</em> to <em>petra</em> is that <em>petra</em> is feminine and cannot
          serve as a man's name without wrecking the pun that is the whole point of the
          sentence.</p>

          <div class="tg-note">
            <span class="tg-note__label">Conceded, and it matters</span>
            <p>The most natural reading of <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 16:18">Matthew
            16:18</a> in its own grammar is that the rock is Peter — Peter as the man who
            has just made that confession. A great many Protestant commentators grant
            this and locate their disagreement further up the stack, at claims 4 through 7.
            That is the honest place for the disagreement to sit. An argument that has to
            win at verse 18 to survive is an argument built on sand.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="keys">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Keys — and Why Isaiah 22 Is the Best Argument</h2>

          <p>This is the single strongest argument for reading Peter's commission as an
          office rather than a personal honour, and it is the one most often skipped by
          Protestant treatments. It deserves to be laid out in full.</p>

          <p>"The keys of the kingdom" is not generic authority language. It is a quotation
          of a specific Old Testament office:</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            On that day I will summon My servant, Eliakim son of Hilkiah. I will clothe him
            with your robe and tie your sash around him. I will put your authority in his
            hand, and he will be a father to the dwellers of Jerusalem and to the house of
            Judah. I will place on his shoulder the key to the house of David. What he opens
            no one can shut, and what he shuts no one can open. I will drive him like a peg
            into a firm place, and he will be a throne of glory for the house of his father.
            <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Isaiah 22:20-23">Isaiah 22:20–23</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>Four correspondences, and they are not loose:</p>

          <div class="tg-point">
            <span class="tg-point__label">The key</span>
            <div class="tg-point__heading">A singular key, held by a singular officer, over
            a house belonging to someone else.</div>
            <p>Eliakim is the royal steward — the <em>al habbayit</em>, the one "over the
            house," who governs the household in the king's name. He does not own the
            house. He runs it.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-point">
            <span class="tg-point__label">Opening and shutting</span>
            <div class="tg-point__heading">The same authority-couplet appears in both texts.</div>
            <p>Isaiah's "what he opens no one can shut" answers to Matthew's binding and
            loosing that stands in heaven. The steward's decisions are ratified above his
            own head.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-point">
            <span class="tg-point__label">Fatherhood</span>
            <div class="tg-point__heading">"He will be a father to the dwellers of Jerusalem."</div>
            <p>The steward's relation to the household is paternal. Whatever one thinks of
            the later title, the Old Testament office it echoes is described in exactly
            this language.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-point">
            <span class="tg-point__label">Succession</span>
            <div class="tg-point__heading">Eliakim receives the office <em>from Shebna</em>.</div>
            <p>This is the heart of the argument. Isaiah 22 is not the institution of an
            office; it is a <strong>transfer</strong> of one. Shebna is deposed
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Isaiah 22:15-19">Isa 22:15–19</a>) and Eliakim
            installed. The office therefore visibly outlives its holder. If Jesus reaches
            for that oracle to describe what he is giving Peter, he is reaching for a
            successive office, not a personal medal.</p>
          </div>

          <p>Add to this the pattern of <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 1:20">Acts 1:20</a>,
          where the apostles fill Judas's place by citing "May another take his position"
          — Greek <em>episkopē</em>, the same root as the word later used for the bishop's
          office. The apostles themselves treated an apostolic place as something that
          could be filled again.</p>

          <div class="tg-note">
            <span class="tg-note__label">State this argument honestly or not at all</span>
            <p>The Isaiah 22 case is not apologetic ingenuity applied after the fact. The
            allusion is real, the vocabulary overlap is dense, and the successive character
            of the Eliakim office is on the page. Any treatment of the papacy that does not
            engage it is arguing against a weaker case than the one that exists. Part II
            answers it — from within Scripture's own use of the same oracle — but it
            answers it as a serious argument.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="shepherd">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Shepherd and Strengthener</h2>

          <h3 class="tg-subsection">"Feed my sheep"</h3>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            Jesus asked a second time, "Simon son of John, do you love Me?" "Yes, Lord," he
            answered, "You know I love You." Jesus told him, "Shepherd My sheep."
            <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="John 21:16">John 21:16</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>Three times — answering the three denials — the risen Christ commissions Peter
          over his flock. The verb in verse 16 is <em>poimainō</em>, to shepherd or rule as
          a shepherd; the object is not "your sheep" but <em>mine</em>. The flock remains
          Christ's; Peter is put over it. Nothing comparable is said to any other disciple
          individually.</p>

          <h3 class="tg-subsection">The prayer for Peter's faith</h3>

          <p>The subtler text, and often missed because English flattens it:</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            Simon, Simon, Satan has asked to sift each of you like wheat. But I have prayed
            for you, Simon, that your faith will not fail. And when you have turned back,
            strengthen your brothers.
            <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 22:31-32">Luke 22:31–32</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>The Greek shifts number mid-saying. Satan demanded <em>all of you</em>
          (plural); Christ prays for <em>you</em>, singular — Peter — that his faith
          specifically may not give out; and the purpose clause is that Peter, once
          restored, will be the one who steadies the rest. A prayer of Christ's for the
          persistence of one man's faith, offered with a view to that man stabilising the
          others, is exactly the shape of what later gets called the Petrine ministry.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="narrative">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Narrative Pattern</h2>

          <p>Beyond the three great texts, the sheer shape of the Gospels and Acts is
          evidence, and it is cumulative rather than decisive at any one point.</p>

          <div class="tg-timeline">
            <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">Four lists</span><span>Peter is named first in <strong>every</strong> apostolic list — <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 10:2">Matt 10:2</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="Mark 3:16">Mark 3:16</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 6:14">Luke 6:14</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 1:13">Acts 1:13</a>. Judas is always last. The order is not alphabetical, chronological, or by age.</span></div>
            <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">Matt 10:2</span><span>"first, Simon, called Peter" — <em>prōtos</em>. In a list of twelve, a word meaning "number one" is redundant; Matthew supplies it anyway.</span></div>
            <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">Acts 1</span><span>Peter convenes the assembly and directs the replacement of Judas.</span></div>
            <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">Acts 2</span><span>Peter preaches at Pentecost — the kingdom opened to Israel.</span></div>
            <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">Acts 8</span><span>Peter (with John) is the apostolic presence when Samaria receives the Spirit.</span></div>
            <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">Acts 10</span><span>Peter is given the vision and opens the door to the Gentiles at Cornelius's house.</span></div>
            <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">Acts 15</span><span>At the Jerusalem council Peter speaks first, and the debate stops when he finishes (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 15:12">v. 12</a>).</span></div>
            <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">1 Cor 15:5</span><span>In the earliest resurrection creed Paul quotes, the risen Christ "appeared to Cephas and then to the Twelve."</span></div>
            <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">Gal 1:18</span><span>Paul's first Jerusalem visit after conversion is specifically to <em>see Cephas</em> — the verb <em>historēsai</em> suggests a visit made to consult a person of consequence.</span></div>
          </div>

          <p>Peter's name occurs more often in the New Testament than any other disciple's
          by a wide margin. He speaks more, is addressed more, is corrected more, and is
          the only disciple whose personal fortunes the narrative keeps tracking. He is
          plainly not one apostle among interchangeable equals, and any account that makes
          him one is not reading the documents.</p>

          <div class="tg-note">
            <span class="tg-note__label">On counting names</span>
            <p>Word-counts are often quoted here without checking. Searching the Berean
            Standard Bible used by this site, "Peter" occurs 181 times in the New Testament
            and "Cephas" 9 more. The raw count for "John" (160) looks comparable but is
            inflated by John the Baptist, the Fourth Gospel's title, and "Simon son of
            John." The qualitative claim — Peter dominates the apostolic narrative — is
            secure; the tidy numerical claims that circulate are usually not, so this study
            rests on the four list-orders instead, which anyone can check in a minute.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="for-history">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Early Historical Case</h2>

          <p>Three historical points belong on this side of the ledger, and Part III
          documents them from the sources.</p>

          <div class="tg-point">
            <span class="tg-point__label">Rome's preeminence is early, not medieval</span>
            <div class="tg-point__heading">By AD 96 the Roman church is intervening in
            another city's internal affairs; by AD 180 Irenaeus is citing Rome as the
            reference standard for apostolic tradition.</div>
            <p>Whatever those facts prove, they are not a later invention retrojected. The
            claim that "Rome was nobody until the Middle Ages" is simply false, and any
            Protestant argument that depends on it is built on a mistake.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-point">
            <span class="tg-point__label">Rome's doctrinal record was good</span>
            <div class="tg-point__heading">In the great controversies, Rome held.</div>
            <p>Rome stood by Nicaea and by Athanasius when much of the East went Arian;
            Leo's <em>Tome</em> supplied the decisive Christological formula at Chalcedon;
            Rome resisted Monothelitism after the Honorius episode. A believer weighing
            this question should notice that the see whose authority is in dispute was, on
            the substance of Christology, right more often than its critics.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-point">
            <span class="tg-point__label">Peter died in Rome</span>
            <div class="tg-point__heading">This is as well attested as most facts of the
            period.</div>
            <p>1 Clement 5 (c. 96) reports Peter's martyrdom; Ignatius writes to Rome as
            the church where Peter and Paul gave commands (c. 107–117); Dionysius of
            Corinth (c. 170) says both apostles taught in Italy and were martyred; and
            Gaius of Rome (c. 200) points to their "trophies" on the Vatican hill and the
            Ostian Way. <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 5:13">1 Peter 5:13</a>'s greeting
            from "Babylon" is very widely read as Rome. Denying claim 2 is a losing
            position and Protestants should stop trying to hold it.</p>
          </div>
        </section>
      </section><!-- /part-1 -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           PART II — THE CASE AGAINST
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-part" id="part-2" aria-labelledby="part-2-h">
        <div class="tg-part__head">
          <span class="tg-part__kicker">Part II</span>
          <h2 class="tg-part__title" id="part-2-h">The Case Against, from the Whole Canon</h2>
          <p class="tg-part__intro">The counter-case is not lexical and it is not an
          argument from silence. It is canonical: when the rest of the New Testament picks
          up the very language of Matthew 16, it distributes what Matthew 16 concentrates
          — and when it picks up the Isaiah 22 oracle, it gives the key to Christ.</p>
          <p class="tg-part__verse"><a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 3:7">Revelation 3:7</a> · <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 18:18">Matthew 18:18</a> · <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 5:1">1 Peter 5:1</a> · <a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 2:11">Galatians 2:11</a></p>
        </div>

        <nav class="tg-toc tg-toc--sticky" aria-label="Part II contents">
          <h2 class="tg-toc__title">In this part</h2>
          <ol class="tg-toc__list">
            <li><a href="#who-holds-the-key">Who Actually Holds the Key of David</a></li>
            <li><a href="#peg">The Rest of the Eliakim Oracle</a></li>
            <li><a href="#distributed">Everything Given to Peter Is Given to the Others</a></li>
            <li><a href="#foundation">Foundation Language Is Corporate</a></li>
            <li><a href="#peter-in-acts">Peter's Own Behaviour in Acts</a></li>
            <li><a href="#paul">Paul on Peter</a></li>
            <li><a href="#peters-letter">Peter's Own Letter</a></li>
            <li><a href="#grain">The Grain of Christ's Teaching on Authority</a></li>
            <li><a href="#silences">The Silences, Weighed</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <section class="tg-section" id="who-holds-the-key">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Who Actually Holds the Key of David</h2>

          <p>Part I made the Isaiah 22 argument at full strength. Here is the answer, and
          it comes from inside the New Testament rather than from outside it.</p>

          <p>The Eliakim oracle is quoted once in the New Testament. Not in Matthew — in
          Revelation. And it is applied to Jesus, with the opening-and-shutting couplet
          intact and in Isaiah's own words:</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            These are the words of the One who is holy and true, who holds the key of David.
            What He opens no one can shut, and what He shuts no one can open.
            <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 3:7">Revelation 3:7</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>Set the two side by side. Isaiah 22:22 — "I will place on his shoulder the key
          to the house of David. What he opens no one can shut, and what he shuts no one can
          open." Revelation 3:7 — "who holds the key of David. What He opens no one can shut,
          and what He shuts no one can open." That is not an allusion; it is a citation, and
          the subject is Christ.</p>

          <p>The same book has already told us who is holding keys and which ones:</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            I was dead, and behold, now I am alive forever and ever! And I hold the keys of
            Death and of Hades.
            <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 1:18">Revelation 1:18</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>Which reaches back and grips <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 16:18">Matthew
          16:18</a> itself: Christ promises that the gates of <em>Hades</em> will not
          prevail — and Revelation says why. He holds that key personally.</p>

          <div class="tg-note">
            <span class="tg-note__label">The Catholic answer to this, which is a real one</span>
            <p>A Catholic reply is available and should be stated: of course Christ holds
            the key — he is the King. Eliakim held the key <em>of the house of David</em>
            while David's heir reigned. A steward's key is always the king's key, held on
            his behalf and in his absence; Revelation 3:7 establishes whose key it is, not
            that no one is ever given it. That is a coherent reading and it is not a dodge.</p>
            <p>The rejoinder is about where the weight falls. If Matthew 16 is deliberately
            invoking the steward office, then the New Testament's own later use of that
            oracle is the best available commentary on it — and that use puts the key back
            in the hands of the King, in the risen Christ's own self-description to seven
            churches that are given no Roman reference point at all. Meanwhile the offices
            the New Testament does describe for the post-apostolic church, in the letters
            written to organise it, are <em>presbyteros</em> and <em>episkopos</em> —
            plural in each city, and never singular over all cities. The steward reading is
            an inference the New Testament had the vocabulary to draw and did not draw.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="peg">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Rest of the Eliakim Oracle</h2>

          <p>The Isaiah 22 argument almost always stops at verse 23 — "I will drive him like
          a peg into a firm place." Read three verses further:</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            So they will hang on him all the glory of his father's house: the descendants and
            the offshoots — all the lesser vessels, from bowls to every kind of jar. In that
            day, declares the LORD of Hosts, the peg driven into a firm place will give way;
            it will be sheared off and fall, and the load upon it will be cut down. Indeed,
            the LORD has spoken.
            <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Isaiah 22:24-25">Isaiah 22:24–25</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>The oracle ends with the peg failing under the weight hung on it. Whatever else
          Isaiah 22 is, it is not a charter of indefectible succession. It is a warning
          about what happens when a household hangs all its glory on one man — bowls, jars,
          descendants, offshoots, the whole family fortune loaded onto a single fixture
          until it shears off the wall.</p>

          <div class="tg-principle">
            <span class="tg-principle__label">The point, stated fairly</span>
            <p class="tg-principle__text">This does not refute the Catholic reading. If
            Christ borrows an image, he is not obliged to import every detail of its
            original context — and the New Testament regularly takes up Old Testament
            figures and transposes them. But it does mean the passage cannot be used as a
            guarantee of permanence while the verses that describe the office collapsing
            are quietly left out. If Isaiah 22 is admissible as evidence for the office, it
            is admissible for its ending too.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="distributed">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Everything Given to Peter Is Given to the Others</h2>

          <p>Take the components of the Matthew 16 commission one at a time and follow them
          through the rest of the Gospels. Each is re-issued in the plural.</p>

          <table class="pp-grade">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Given to Peter</th><th>Given to the others</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Binding and loosing<br><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 16:19">Matt 16:19</a></td>
                <td>"Truly I tell you, whatever <strong>you</strong> bind on earth will be bound in heaven" — plural, to the disciples, in the context of church discipline, with the church itself as the final court (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 18:17-18">Matt 18:17–18</a>).</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Shepherding Christ's flock<br><a class="ref" data-ref="John 21:16">John 21:16</a></td>
                <td>The identical charge to the Ephesian elders — plural — "Be shepherds of the church of God" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 20:28">Acts 20:28</a>), and to elders generally (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 5:2">1 Pet 5:2</a>), by Peter.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Remitting sins (implied in the keys)</td>
                <td>"If you forgive anyone his sins, they are forgiven" — spoken to the assembled disciples (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 20:22-23">John 20:22–23</a>).</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Foundation of the church<br><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 16:18">Matt 16:18</a></td>
                <td>"Built on the foundation of the apostles and prophets" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 2:20">Eph 2:20</a>); twelve foundations bearing twelve names (<a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 21:14">Rev 21:14</a>).</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>A throne in the kingdom</td>
                <td>Twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes — promised to all of them in the same breath as the rebuke about greatness (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 22:29-30">Luke 22:29–30</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 19:28">Matt 19:28</a>).</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <p>The pattern is consistent: whatever is uniquely Peter's, none of the
          <em>powers</em> are. What remains unique is his priority — he is first, he is
          named, he goes ahead. That is a primacy of initiative and witness. It is not
          obviously a jurisdiction, and the New Testament never turns it into one.</p>

          <h3 class="tg-subsection">The context of Luke 22 is usually cut off</h3>

          <p>Part I quoted "I have prayed for you, Simon." Read the verses immediately
          before it:</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            A dispute also arose among the disciples as to which of them would be considered
            the greatest. So Jesus declared, "The kings of the Gentiles lord it over them,
            and those in authority over them call themselves benefactors. But you shall not
            be like them. Instead, the greatest among you should be like the youngest, and
            the one who leads like the one who serves… And I bestow on you a kingdom, just as
            My Father has bestowed one on Me, so that you may eat and drink at My table in My
            kingdom, and sit on thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel. Simon, Simon,
            Satan has asked to sift each of you like wheat…"
            <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 22:24-31">Luke 22:24–31</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>The prayer for Peter is spoken four verses after Christ forbade the ranking
          question and one verse after he conferred the kingdom on all twelve. Whatever
          "strengthen your brothers" means, Luke has framed it so that it cannot mean
          "you are the greatest" — because Luke has just recorded Jesus refusing that
          question when it was asked directly. And the very next thing Jesus says to Peter
          is that he will deny him three times before morning.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="foundation">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Foundation Language Is Corporate</h2>

          <p>Three texts, each from a different author, each using building language about
          the church, and none of them singular:</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            Therefore you are no longer strangers and foreigners, but fellow citizens with
            the saints and members of God's household, built on the foundation of the
            apostles and prophets, with Christ Jesus Himself as the cornerstone.
            <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 2:19-20">Ephesians 2:19–20</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            By the grace God has given me, I laid a foundation as an expert builder… For no
            one can lay a foundation other than the one already laid, which is Jesus Christ.
            <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 3:10-11">1 Corinthians 3:10–11</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            The wall of the city had twelve foundations bearing the names of the twelve
            apostles of the Lamb.
            <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 21:14">Revelation 21:14</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>John's vision of the consummated church is the strongest of the three, because
          it is precisely the moment when a single Petrine foundation would be visible if
          there were one. There are twelve, and they are equal courses in one wall.</p>

          <div class="tg-note">
            <span class="tg-note__label">The counter, granted</span>
            <p>A foundation of twelve does not exclude one of the twelve having a role
            among them, any more than a college of apostles excludes a spokesman. Ephesians
            2:20 and Matthew 16:18 are not in contradiction; Peter can be the rock
            <em>within</em> an apostolic foundation. What these texts do establish is the
            <em>category</em>: foundations are laid once, by the apostolic generation, and
            the New Testament's own picture of the finished building has twelve names on it
            and no thirteenth course added later.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="peter-in-acts">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Peter's Own Behaviour in Acts</h2>

          <p>Acts is the one book that shows the apostolic church actually governing itself.
          Peter is prominent throughout the first half — and in five specific places he does
          not behave as a monarch would.</p>

          <div class="tg-point">
            <span class="tg-point__label">He is sent</span>
            <div class="tg-point__heading"><a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 8:14">Acts 8:14</a>
            — "the apostles in Jerusalem… sent Peter and John."</div>
            <p>Whatever else is true of an office with universal immediate jurisdiction, its
            holder is not dispatched by a committee. In Luke's grammar, the apostles are the
            subject and Peter is the object.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-point">
            <span class="tg-point__label">He is called to account, and answers</span>
            <div class="tg-point__heading"><a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 11:2-4">Acts
            11:2–4</a> — the circumcised believers "took issue with him," and Peter
            "explained."</div>
            <p>He does not invoke his commission. He gives a defence, and it is
            <em>accepted</em> (v. 18) — which is to say, ratified by the body. That is how a
            colleague behaves, not a sovereign.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-point">
            <span class="tg-point__label">James renders the verdict</span>
            <div class="tg-point__heading"><a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 15:19">Acts
            15:19</a> — "It is my judgment, therefore…"</div>
            <p>At the council Peter's testimony is decisive in the debate — Part I is right
            about that. But the summing-up, the scriptural warrant, and the ruling come from
            James; and the decree goes out in the name of "the apostles and elders, with the
            whole church" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 15:22">v. 22</a>) under the formula
            "It seemed good to the Holy Spirit and to us"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 15:28">v. 28</a>). That is a conciliar decision
            procedure, and it is the only one the New Testament shows us.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-point">
            <span class="tg-point__label">He reports to James</span>
            <div class="tg-point__heading"><a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 12:17">Acts
            12:17</a> — "Send word to James and to the brothers."</div>
            <p>Freed from prison, Peter's instruction is to notify James — and then he
            leaves. From that point Luke's Jerusalem narrative belongs to James
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 21:18">Acts 21:18</a>: "Paul went in with us to
            see James, and all the elders were present" — Peter is not mentioned).</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-point">
            <span class="tg-point__label">He refuses obeisance</span>
            <div class="tg-point__heading"><a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 10:25-26">Acts
            10:25–26</a> — "Stand up. I am only a man myself."</div>
            <p>A small scene, and easy to over-read. But it is the one moment in Acts where
            someone kneels to Peter, and Peter's instinct is to get him off the floor.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="tg-subsection">And the one succession Acts does record</h3>

          <p>Acts 1 is cited by the Catholic side as proof that apostolic places can be
          filled again — fairly, since that is what happens. But read the qualification
          the apostles set:</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            Therefore it is necessary to choose one of the men who have accompanied us the
            whole time the Lord Jesus went in and out among us, beginning from John's baptism
            until the day Jesus was taken up from us. For one of these must become a witness
            with us of His resurrection.
            <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 1:21-22">Acts 1:21–22</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>Two features cut against using this as a succession template. The requirement
          is <strong>eyewitness of the entire ministry and of the resurrection</strong> —
          a qualification that expires with that generation and cannot be transmitted. And
          the vacancy exists because of <strong>defection</strong>: Judas abandoned his place.
          When an apostle simply dies — James the son of Zebedee, executed in
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 12:2">Acts 12:2</a> — nobody is appointed to replace
          him, and Luke does not treat this as a problem needing a solution.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="paul">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Paul on Peter</h2>

          <p>Galatians 1–2 is Paul's own account of his relations with the Jerusalem
          apostles, written to establish that his gospel did not come from them. It is the
          most direct evidence we have about how an apostle regarded Peter's standing.</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            But as for the highly esteemed — whatever they were makes no difference to me;
            God does not show favoritism — those leaders added nothing to me. On the
            contrary, they saw that I had been entrusted to preach the gospel to the
            uncircumcised, just as Peter had been to the circumcised… And recognizing the
            grace that I had been given, James, Cephas, and John — those reputed to be
            pillars — gave me and Barnabas the right hand of fellowship.
            <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 2:6-9">Galatians 2:6–9</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>Four things in this passage:</p>

          <ul>
            <li><strong>Parallel commissions, not a chain of command.</strong> Peter to the
            circumcised, Paul to the Gentiles — an apportionment of mission between peers.
            Paul does not describe himself as licensed by Peter.</li>
            <li><strong>Pillars, plural — and James is named first.</strong> If there is a
            rank order in Paul's phrasing, Peter is not at the top of it.</li>
            <li><strong>"Added nothing to me."</strong> The Jerusalem leadership had no
            content to supply and, in Paul's telling, no authority to withhold approval.</li>
            <li><strong>And then the rebuke.</strong></li>
          </ul>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            When Cephas came to Antioch, however, I opposed him to his face, because he stood
            to be condemned… When I saw that they were not walking in line with the truth of
            the gospel, I said to Cephas in front of them all…
            <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 2:11-14">Galatians 2:11–14</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>Paul corrects Peter publicly, on a matter of the gospel's integrity, and writes
          it into a circular letter for churches to read. He does not appeal to Peter; he
          confronts him. And the confrontation is not treated as insubordination requiring
          justification — it is offered as evidence of Paul's own apostolic standing.</p>

          <div class="tg-note">
            <span class="tg-note__label">Stated fairly</span>
            <p>This does not disprove a Petrine primacy. Catholic readers have always
            observed, correctly, that Peter's error at Antioch was in <em>conduct</em>, not
            in doctrinal definition, and that the later doctrine of infallibility is
            expressly limited to defining acts and would not be touched by it. Popes have
            been rebuked by saints throughout the tradition. What Galatians 2 does show is
            that in the apostolic era, a peer's public correction of Peter — on the truth of
            the gospel, before the whole congregation — was a normal and defensible thing
            to do, and Paul expects his readers to think so too.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="peters-letter">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Peter's Own Letter</h2>

          <p>If any document in the New Testament might be expected to reflect a Petrine
          office, it is the letter Peter wrote to churches across five provinces of Asia
          Minor. Here is how he identifies himself in relation to their leaders:</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            As a fellow elder, a witness of Christ's sufferings, and a partaker of the glory
            to be revealed, I appeal to the elders among you: Be shepherds of God's flock
            that is among you, watching over them not out of compulsion, but because it is
            God's will; not out of greed, but out of eagerness; not lording it over those
            entrusted to you, but being examples to the flock. And when the Chief Shepherd
            appears, you will receive the crown of glory that will never fade away.
            <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 5:1-4">1 Peter 5:1–4</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>Four words are doing very heavy work.</p>

          <div class="tg-definition">
            <span class="tg-definition__term">Fellow elder</span>
            <span class="tg-definition__original">συμπρεσβύτερος (sympresbyteros)</span>
            <span class="tg-definition__def">Not "chief of the elders" but the
            <em>syn-</em> compound: alongside, together with. Peter places himself in the
            same rank as the men he is addressing. He had the vocabulary for hierarchy and
            he used the vocabulary for solidarity.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-definition">
            <span class="tg-definition__term">Not lording it over</span>
            <span class="tg-definition__original">μὴ κατακυριεύοντες (mē katakyrieuontes)</span>
            <span class="tg-definition__def">The compound verb Jesus used of the Gentile
            rulers in <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 20:25">Matthew 20:25</a> —
            <em>katakyrieuousin</em>, "lord it over them" — now negated and applied by Peter
            to pastoral office. Peter is quoting the Lord's prohibition and legislating it
            for church leaders.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-definition">
            <span class="tg-definition__term">Chief Shepherd</span>
            <span class="tg-definition__original">ἀρχιποίμην (archipoimēn)</span>
            <span class="tg-definition__def">The only "chief shepherd" in the letter is
            Christ, who has not yet appeared. Elders are under-shepherds awaiting his
            return. There is no intermediate tier.</span>
          </div>

          <p>And earlier in the same letter, Peter takes the stone-and-building imagery of
          Matthew 16 and hands it to everybody:</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            As you come to Him, the living stone, rejected by men but chosen and precious in
            God's sight, you also, like living stones, are being built into a spiritual house
            to be a holy priesthood… "See, I lay in Zion a stone, a chosen and precious
            cornerstone."
            <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 2:4-6">1 Peter 2:4–6</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>The man whom Christ called <em>kepha</em> writes a letter about stones and
          building in which Christ is the living stone and the cornerstone, and every
          believer is a living stone in the wall. His own name is not in the metaphor.</p>

          <div class="tg-principle">
            <span class="tg-principle__label">The single hardest datum for claim 6</span>
            <p class="tg-principle__text">The office claims universal, ordinary, immediate
            jurisdiction on the basis of what Christ gave Peter. Peter's own surviving
            letter addresses churches he did not found, in the one place where he had every
            occasion to speak as their superior, and he calls himself their colleague and
            forbids to church leaders precisely the posture the office later assumed. That
            is not silence; it is testimony in the opposite direction, from the only
            witness whose testimony would be decisive.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="grain">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Grain of Christ's Teaching on Authority</h2>

          <p>The objection here is not merely that the papacy is absent from Scripture. It
          is that Scripture's explicit teaching about power in the church runs in a
          particular direction, and the direction is inversion.</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            You know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and their superiors
            exercise authority over them. It shall not be this way among you. Instead,
            whoever wants to become great among you must be your servant, and whoever wants
            to be first among you must be your slave — just as the Son of Man did not come
            to be served, but to serve, and to give His life as a ransom for many.
            <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 20:25-28">Matthew 20:25–28</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            But you are not to be called "Rabbi," for you have one Teacher, and you are all
            brothers. And do not call anyone on earth your father, for you have one Father,
            who is in heaven. Nor are you to be called instructors, for you have one
            Instructor, the Christ. The greatest among you shall be your servant.
            <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 23:8-11">Matthew 23:8–11</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>And the headship texts are unambiguous about who occupies the position:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>"God… made Him head over everything for the church, which is His body"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 1:22-23">Eph 1:22–23</a>).</li>
            <li>"He is the head of the body, the church… so that in all things He may have
            preeminence" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Colossians 1:18">Col 1:18</a>).</li>
            <li>"When the Chief Shepherd appears"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 5:4">1 Pet 5:4</a>).</li>
          </ul>

          <div class="tg-note">
            <span class="tg-note__label">Where this argument is weaker than it sounds</span>
            <p>Matthew 23:9 cannot mean that the word "father" is forbidden, or Paul broke
            it in the next generation ("I became your father through the gospel,"
            <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 4:15">1 Cor 4:15</a>), and Stephen breaks
            it in <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 7:2">Acts 7:2</a>. The prohibition is
            against a <em>class</em> of honorific that relocates to a man what belongs to
            God. Used as a knock-down argument against the title "pope," it proves too much
            and collapses. Used as evidence of the direction of Christ's teaching about
            ecclesial rank, it is sound. Take it for what it is.</p>
            <p>The same care applies to Matthew 20:25–28. Christ is not abolishing office —
            he says "whoever wants to be <em>first</em>," which presumes someone will be.
            He is redefining what being first consists of. A defender of the papacy can say,
            entirely consistently, that <em>servus servorum Dei</em> is exactly this
            teaching institutionalised. The force of the objection is not that leadership is
            forbidden but that a claim to ordinary, immediate jurisdiction over every
            baptised person on earth is a strange thing to grow out of this soil.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="silences">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Silences, Weighed</h2>

          <p>Arguments from silence are only as good as the expectation behind them. Here
          are the New Testament's silences, ranked by how much they should count.</p>

          <table class="pp-grade">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>The silence</th><th>How much weight it bears</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>No text says Peter's primacy passes to a successor.</td>
                <td><strong>Considerable.</strong> The Pastoral Epistles exist precisely to
                arrange the church's leadership after the apostles — qualifications,
                appointments, transmission of teaching
                (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 2:2">2 Tim 2:2</a>;
                <a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 1:5">Titus 1:5</a>). They set out elders and
                overseers and deacons, and nothing above them.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>No text identifies Rome as Peter's see, or any see as inheriting his place.</td>
                <td><strong>Considerable.</strong> Antioch also claimed Peter as founder,
                with early support; the New Testament arbitrates between neither because it
                shows no awareness of the question.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Romans 16 greets some twenty-six people by name and does not mention Peter.</td>
                <td><strong>Moderate.</strong> Real, but explicable — Peter may simply not
                have been there when Paul wrote (c. AD 57), which is also consistent with
                Paul's stated policy of not building "on someone else's foundation"
                (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 15:20">Rom 15:20</a>) — a policy he would
                have been breaking by writing Romans at all if Peter had already founded
                that church.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Revelation's letters go to seven churches of Asia, with no Roman reference point.</td>
                <td><strong>Moderate.</strong> Christ addresses seven congregations
                individually, corrects them individually, and appoints no earthly
                clearing-house. But the book's genre and geography limit the inference.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>No New Testament church has a single bishop over its elders.</td>
                <td><strong>Considerable — and this one is positive evidence, not silence.</strong>
                Paul summons "the elders" of Ephesus and calls those same men "overseers"
                (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 20:17">Acts 20:17</a>,
                <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 20:28">28</a>); Titus is to appoint "elders in
                every town," then describes the qualifications of "an overseer"
                (<a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 1:5-7">Titus 1:5–7</a>); Philippians is
                addressed to "the overseers and deacons," plural, in one city
                (<a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 1:1">Phil 1:1</a>). Claim 3 — Peter as
                monarchical bishop of Rome — requires an office that did not yet exist in
                that form anywhere.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <div class="tg-note">
            <span class="tg-note__label">The honest counter-silence</span>
            <p>Protestants owe the same test in return. The New Testament also contains no
            table of contents, no doctrine of the canon, no word <em>Trinity</em>, and no
            statement that Scripture alone is the final authority in the form the
            Reformation asserted. If "not explicit in the text, therefore not binding" is
            the rule, it takes down more than the papacy. Part IV takes this seriously
            rather than waving at it, because it is the strongest card the other side holds.</p>
          </div>
        </section>
      </section><!-- /part-2 -->

