        <section class="tg-section" id="h-clement">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">AD 96 · 1 Clement</h2>

          <p>The Roman church writes to Corinth about a leadership dispute, in a notably
          firm tone. It is the earliest Christian document outside the New Testament that
          shows one church addressing another's internal affairs.</p>

          <p><strong>For Rome:</strong> the letter is unmistakably authoritative in tone,
          and Irenaeus later cites it as an act of the Roman church. It also contains the
          earliest explicit doctrine of ministerial succession:</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            Our apostles also knew, through our Lord Jesus Christ, that there would be strife
            on account of the office of the episcopate. For this reason, therefore, inasmuch
            as they had obtained a perfect fore-knowledge of this, they appointed those
            [ministers] already mentioned, and afterwards gave instructions, that when these
            should fall asleep, other approved men should succeed them in their ministry.
            <cite>1 Clement 44 (Roberts–Donaldson, <em>Ante-Nicene Fathers</em> I) · <a href="../../library/read/clement-first-epistle/">read in the Library</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p><strong>Against a papal reading:</strong> four things, all on the face of the
          document.</p>

          <ul>
            <li><strong>No author, no title, no office invoked.</strong> The letter opens:
            "The church of God which sojourns at Rome, to the church of God sojourning at
            Corinth." Clement never names himself. There is no "I, Clement, bishop of
            Rome"; there is a congregation writing to a congregation.</li>
            <li><strong>It argues from Scripture and from order</strong> — at length, with
            examples from Israel's history and even from the Roman army — not from a right
            to command.</li>
            <li><strong>Bishops and presbyters are the same men.</strong> In the passage
            just quoted, those who are ejected from "the episcopate" are, two sentences
            later, "those presbyters who, having finished their course…" The words are used
            interchangeably, exactly as in the New Testament.</li>
            <li><strong>Appointment is congregational.</strong> Successors are installed
            "with the consent of the whole church."</li>
          </ul>

          <div class="tg-note">
            <span class="tg-note__label">A precision worth keeping</span>
            <p>1 Clement 5 is regularly cited as proof that Peter died in Rome. What it
            actually says is that Peter "endured not one or two, but numerous labours; and
            when he had at length suffered martyrdom, departed to the place of glory due to
            him." No city is named — for Peter or, in so many words, for Paul. The
            inference to Rome is a good one (the letter is Roman, and the two apostles are
            held up as the local generation's own examples), but it is an inference. The
            explicit locating evidence comes later, from Dionysius of Corinth and Gaius.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="h-ignatius">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">c. 107–117 · Ignatius of Antioch</h2>

          <p>Ignatius is our great early witness to the threefold ministry. He writes seven
          letters on his way to martyrdom, and in six of them he presses obedience to the
          local bishop relentlessly — "do nothing without the bishop."</p>

          <p><strong>For Rome:</strong> his greeting to the Roman church is extraordinary
          among the seven:</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            …the church which presides in the place of the region of the Romans, worthy of
            God, worthy of honour, worthy of the highest happiness, worthy of praise… and
            which presides over love.
            <cite>Ignatius, <em>To the Romans</em>, salutation (ANF I) · <a href="../../library/read/ignatius-romans/">read in the Library</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>"Presides over love" — Greek <em>prokathēmenē tēs agapēs</em> — is read by
          Catholic scholars as an early acknowledgement of Roman primacy, and by others as
          preeminence <em>in</em> charity: Rome was famously generous with alms, a
          reputation Dionysius of Corinth attests around AD 170.</p>

          <p><strong>Against:</strong> two facts, both striking.</p>

          <ul>
            <li><strong>The letter to Rome is the only one of the seven that never mentions
            a bishop there.</strong> The man whose entire programme is submission to the
            bishop writes to Rome and has no bishop to name. On the evidence of Ignatius
            himself, Rome in c. 110 does not yet have the monarchical episcopate that
            Antioch and the Asian churches already have.</li>
            <li><strong>He names Peter <em>and</em> Paul, together, as the church's
            apostolic authorities</strong> — "I do not, as Peter and Paul, issue commandments
            unto you. They were apostles; I am but a condemned man." Rome's apostolic
            dignity, at its earliest attestation, is a joint one.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="h-irenaeus">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">c. 180 · Irenaeus, <em>Against Heresies</em> III.3</h2>

          <p>This is the pivot text of the whole debate, and it is genuinely contested. The
          Greek original is lost; we have a Latin translation whose key phrase is ambiguous.</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            Since, however, it would be very tedious, in such a volume as this, to reckon up
            the successions of all the churches, we do put to confusion all those who…
            assemble in unauthorized meetings; [we do this, I say,] by indicating that
            tradition derived from the apostles, of the very great, the very ancient, and
            universally known church founded and organized at Rome by the two most glorious
            apostles, Peter and Paul… For it is a matter of necessity that every church
            should agree with this church, on account of its pre-eminent authority, that is,
            the faithful everywhere, inasmuch as the apostolical tradition has been preserved
            continuously by those [faithful men] who exist everywhere.
            <cite>Irenaeus, <em>Against Heresies</em> III.3.2 (ANF I) · <a href="../../library/read/irenaeus-against-heresies/">read in the Library</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p><strong>For Rome:</strong> this is the strongest early evidence there is. A
          bishop in Gaul, writing about a doctrinal test, names Rome as the church with
          which every church must agree, gives its succession list from Linus onward, and
          grounds this in its apostolic origin. It is not nothing, and the common Protestant
          move of explaining it away is unconvincing.</p>

          <p><strong>Against a jurisdictional reading:</strong> three points, the first two
          from the passage itself.</p>

          <ul>
            <li><strong>Irenaeus's own argument is that <em>any</em> apostolic church would
            serve.</strong> He says explicitly that listing all the successions would be
            "very tedious" and that he therefore takes Rome as his example. He then cites
            Smyrna and Polycarp, and Ephesus, in the same demonstration. Rome is the best
            available specimen of a universal rule, not the rule itself.</li>
            <li><strong>The key phrase is ambiguous in the Latin.</strong> <em>Convenire ad</em>
            can mean "agree with" or "resort to"; <em>potentiorem principalitatem</em> can be
            "more powerful authority" or "more excellent origin." The ANF rendering above
            takes one option; the sentence has been construed both ways by competent
            scholars for four centuries. A doctrine of universal jurisdiction should not
            rest on a disputed clause in a translation of a lost original.</li>
            <li><strong>The succession is of teaching, not of jurisdiction.</strong> The
            whole context is a test for identifying apostolic doctrine against Gnostic
            claims to secret tradition. It is an epistemological argument, not a
            constitutional one.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="h-victor">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">c. 190 · Victor and the Easter Controversy</h2>

          <p>Victor of Rome attempted to cut off the churches of Asia from communion over
          the date of Easter. Eusebius preserves the episode.</p>

          <p><strong>For Rome:</strong> this is an exercise of something very like universal
          jurisdiction, and it is early. A bishop of Rome believed he could excommunicate
          entire provinces. Whatever else is true, the claim is not a medieval fabrication.</p>

          <p><strong>Against:</strong> the outcome. Polycrates of Ephesus refused, citing
          the tradition of the Asian churches and their own apostolic graves. Other bishops,
          Irenaeus prominent among them, rebuked Victor sharply and urged him "to consider
          the things of peace, and of neighborly unity and love." The excommunication did
          not stick, and communion continued. Eusebius records that the parties "parted from
          each other in peace, both those who observed, and those who did not, maintaining
          the peace of the whole church."</p>

          <div class="tg-principle">
            <span class="tg-principle__label">The pattern this episode establishes</span>
            <p class="tg-principle__text">The claim existed early. Acceptance of the claim
            did not. That distinction runs through the next thousand years and is the single
            most important thing to hold on to when reading the record: evidence that Rome
            <em>asserted</em> authority is not evidence that the church <em>recognised</em>
            it, and the sources very often preserve both the assertion and the refusal in
            the same paragraph.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="h-cyprian">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">c. 250s · Cyprian of Carthage and Firmilian</h2>

          <p>Cyprian is the hardest case for the Catholic side, and the textual situation
          has to be described honestly because it cuts both ways.</p>

          <h3 class="tg-subsection">The two recensions of <em>De Unitate</em> 4</h3>

          <p>Chapter 4 of <em>On the Unity of the Church</em> survives in two forms. One —
          the "Primacy Text" — contains phrases about the primacy given to Peter and the
          chair of Peter. The other, the primitive text, does not. Most modern scholarship,
          Catholic included, holds that both may derive from Cyprian himself, the longer
          being an earlier draft later revised. The text printed in the standard English
          <em>Ante-Nicene Fathers</em> edition — the one in this site's Library — is the
          primitive recension:</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            And although to all the apostles, after His resurrection, He gives an equal power…
            yet, that He might set forth unity, He arranged by His authority the origin of
            that unity, as beginning from one. Assuredly the rest of the apostles were also
            the same as was Peter, endowed with a like partnership both of honour and power;
            but the beginning proceeds from unity.
            <cite>Cyprian, <em>On the Unity of the Church</em> 4 (ANF V, primitive recension) · <a href="../../library/read/cyprian-on-unity/">read in the Library</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>This is Cyprian's actual doctrine and it is coherent: the chair of Peter is a
          symbol of the <em>oneness</em> of the episcopate, and every bishop sits in it.
          "The episcopate is one, each part of which is held by each one for the whole"
          (ch. 5). Peter's priority is a picture of unity, not a grant of superiority.</p>

          <h3 class="tg-subsection">And then his practice</h3>

          <p>Whatever one makes of the recensions, Cyprian's conduct is unambiguous. He
          defied Pope Stephen outright over the rebaptism of heretics, and opened the
          Council of Carthage in September 256 — eighty-seven bishops present — with a
          statement that could not be plainer:</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            For neither does any of us set himself up as a bishop of bishops, nor by tyrannical
            terror does any compel his colleague to the necessity of obedience; since every
            bishop, according to the allowance of his liberty and power, has his own proper
            right of judgment, and can no more be judged by another than he himself can judge
            another.
            <cite>Cyprian, opening address, Seventh Council of Carthage (256) — <em>Sententiae Episcoporum</em>, preface (ANF V; Latin in CSEL 3.1, ed. Hartel)</cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>And Firmilian of Caesarea, writing to Cyprian in the same controversy, treated
          Stephen's appeal to succession from Peter as not merely wrong but absurd, and
          accused him of cutting himself off from unity by his own act.</p>

          <div class="tg-note">
            <span class="tg-note__label">What this does and does not show</span>
            <p>It does not show that Rome had no special standing — Cyprian himself calls
            Rome "the chief church whence priestly unity takes its source" in another
            letter, and he corresponded with Rome constantly. What it shows is that in the
            mid-third century a major bishop, backed by a council of eighty-seven, could
            regard a Roman claim to bind other bishops as an overreach, say so publicly,
            act on it, and remain a saint of the church. That is not compatible with claim
            6 being recognised doctrine in 256.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="h-nicaea">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">325–381 · Nicaea and Constantinople</h2>

          <p>Two conciliar canons, quoted in full because their plain sense is the argument.</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            Let the ancient customs in Egypt, Libya, and Pentapolis prevail, that the Bishop
            of Alexandria have jurisdiction in all these, since the like is customary for the
            Bishop of Rome also. Likewise in Antioch and the other provinces, let the
            churches retain their privileges.
            <cite>Council of Nicaea (325), Canon VI · <a href="../../library/nicaea-i/">read in the Library</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>Read plainly: Rome's authority is <em>regional</em> ("the like is customary"),
          <em>customary</em> rather than divinely instituted, and listed alongside two peers
          as an illustration of a rule Alexandria also enjoys. The first ecumenical council
          describes Rome's jurisdiction, and describes it as one of three.</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            The Bishop of Constantinople, however, shall have the prerogative of honor after
            the Bishop of Rome; because Constantinople is New Rome.
            <cite>Council of Constantinople (381), Canon III · <a href="../../library/constantinople-i/">read in the Library</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>Constantinople is elevated to second place on an explicitly political rationale
          — it is the new imperial capital. If Rome's own rank derived from Peter, this is a
          very strange way for an ecumenical council to reason about the rank immediately
          below it.</p>

          <div class="tg-note">
            <span class="tg-note__label">Fairness on Sardica</span>
            <p>The Catholic side has a real counterweight in this period. The Council of
            Sardica (343/4) granted, in canons 3–5, that a deposed bishop could appeal to
            the bishop of Rome for review — the first formal legal recognition of a Roman
            appellate jurisdiction beyond its own region. Rome also received and vindicated
            Athanasius when the East had condemned him, and Athanasius was right.</p>
            <p>Two qualifications belong with it. Sardica was a Western council whose canons
            the East largely did not receive. And the canons later caused a scandal: when
            Rome cited them to the African bishops in the Apiarius affair (417–419) it cited
            them <em>as canons of Nicaea</em>. The Africans checked their own copies of
            Nicaea, wrote to Alexandria and Constantinople for authenticated texts, found
            the canons were not there, and told Rome so.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="h-chalcedon">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">431–451 · Ephesus, Leo, and Chalcedon</h2>

          <p>The fifth century is where the strongest papal claims and the strongest
          counter-evidence sit in the same room.</p>

          <h3 class="tg-subsection">The high-water marks</h3>

          <ul>
            <li><strong>Ephesus (431).</strong> The papal legate Philip declared to the
            council that "the holy and most blessed Peter, the prince and head of the
            Apostles… received from Our Lord… the keys of the kingdom… who, down to this day,
            and for evermore, lives and exercises judgment in his successors." That is claim
            4 and claim 5 stated explicitly, in a council, in 431.</li>
            <li><strong>Leo the Great (440–461)</strong> articulated Petrine succession more
            fully than anyone before him, preaching that Peter should be regarded as present
            in the person of his unworthy heir, and building the theology of the office on
            Roman legal concepts of inheritance.</li>
            <li><strong>Chalcedon (451).</strong> Leo's <em>Tome</em> was read and acclaimed:
            "Peter has spoken through Leo." Rome supplied the formula that settled the
            Christological question for the whole church.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="tg-subsection">And the counter-evidence, from the same council</h3>

          <ul>
            <li>The bishops did not simply receive the <em>Tome</em> on Leo's authority.
            They examined it against Cyril of Alexandria, whose orthodoxy was the agreed
            standard; a group of Illyrian and Palestinian bishops raised objections that had
            to be answered before it was accepted. The acclamation is a verdict about the
            content, reached after scrutiny — "Peter has spoken through Leo" because what
            Leo said was found to be true.</li>
            <li>The same council passed Canon XXVIII — over Rome's protest — and its
            reasoning is the sharpest datum in the whole record:</li>
          </ul>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            For the Fathers rightly granted prerogatives to the throne of old Rome, because it
            was the royal city. And the One Hundred and Fifty most religious Bishops, actuated
            by the same consideration, gave equal prerogatives to the most holy throne of New
            Rome, justly judging that the city which is honored with the Sovereignty and the
            Senate… should in ecclesiastical matters also be magnified as she is, and rank next
            after her.
            <cite>Council of Chalcedon (451), Canon XXVIII · <a href="../../library/chalcedon-451/">read in the Library</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>An ecumenical council states, as its explicit rationale, that Rome's
          prerogatives were granted <em>by the Fathers</em> and <em>because Rome was the
          imperial city</em>. Not because of Peter. Rome objected; Leo refused to confirm
          the canon; the canon stood in the East regardless.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="h-gregory">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">c. 590–604 · Gregory the Great</h2>

          <p>When the patriarch of Constantinople took the title "ecumenical" — universal —
          patriarch, Gregory of Rome objected in the strongest terms available to him:
          whoever calls himself, or desires to be called, universal priest is in his elation
          the forerunner of Antichrist. He refused the title for Constantinople and refused
          it for himself, preferring <em>servus servorum Dei</em>, servant of the servants
          of God — a formula the papacy has used ever since.</p>

          <div class="tg-note">
            <span class="tg-note__label">In fairness, both ways</span>
            <p>This is often deployed as a knockout and it is not one. Gregory also
            exercised very considerable authority over other sees, intervened widely, and
            could speak of the see of Peter as having a care extending over the whole
            church. His objection was to a specific title he read as implying that other
            bishops were not truly bishops — which is not identical to the 1870 definition.</p>
            <p>What remains, and is not neutralised, is that the greatest pope of the
            patristic West thought the phrase "universal bishop" was a title no Christian
            minister should bear. Whether or not that touches <em>Pastor Aeternus</em>
            technically, it tells you what the office understood itself to be in 595.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="h-honorius">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">638–681 · Honorius</h2>

          <p>Pope Honorius I, in correspondence with Sergius of Constantinople, endorsed
          language later judged Monothelite. Forty-three years after his death, an
          ecumenical council condemned him by name:</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            And with these we define that there shall be expelled from the holy Church of God
            and anathematized Honorius who was some time Pope of Old Rome, because of what we
            found written by him to Sergius, that in all respects he followed his view and
            confirmed his impious doctrines.
            <cite>Third Council of Constantinople (681), Session XIII</cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>Pope Leo II confirmed the council's acts and, in his letter of confirmation,
          charged Honorius with having failed to sanctify the apostolic church with the
          teaching of apostolic tradition and with having permitted its purity to be
          polluted by profane treachery.</p>

          <p><strong>The standard Catholic reply</strong> is internally coherent and should
          be stated: Honorius was condemned for negligence — for failing to extinguish the
          flame — rather than for defining heresy; his letters were private correspondence,
          not <em>ex cathedra</em> acts; and under the 1870 conditions they would not
          qualify. That is a fair reading of the letters.</p>

          <p><strong>What it does not remove</strong> is the shape of the episode. An
          ecumenical council judged a pope, and a pope ratified the judgment. The anathema
          against Honorius was then written into the profession of faith that each new pope
          swore in the <em>Liber Diurnus</em> from roughly the seventh century to the
          eleventh, and it survived in the lessons for the feast of Leo II in the Roman
          Breviary until it was removed. For four centuries, part of becoming pope was
          anathematising a predecessor.</p>

          <div class="tg-note">
            <span class="tg-note__label">And Honorius is not alone</span>
            <p>Two more cases belong here for completeness. <strong>Vigilius</strong>
            (537–555) changed position repeatedly in the Three Chapters controversy, was
            excommunicated by an African synod, and finally submitted to the Second Council
            of Constantinople. <strong>Zosimus</strong> (417–418) initially pronounced
            Caelestius and Pelagius orthodox and reversed himself only after the African
            bishops pressed him hard. Augustine's famous line about that affair —
            <em>causa finita est</em>, "the case is closed" (Sermon 131.10) — is regularly
            quoted as "Rome has spoken, the case is closed." Augustine did not write those
            first three words.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="h-forgeries">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">9th–15th c. · The Forgeries</h2>

          <p>The medieval consolidation of papal authority rested substantially on two
          bodies of documents now universally acknowledged to be forgeries.</p>

          <div class="tg-timeline">
            <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">c. 850</span><span><strong>The Pseudo-Isidorian Decretals</strong> — a large collection of fabricated papal letters, purportedly from the earliest centuries, asserting sweeping Roman jurisdiction and the right of appeal to Rome over the heads of metropolitans. Cited as authentic for seven hundred years.</span></div>
            <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">c. 750–850</span><span><strong>The Donation of Constantine</strong> — a forged imperial grant of primacy over the other patriarchates and temporal sovereignty in the West.</span></div>
            <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">1433 / 1440</span><span>Exposed as forgeries — Nicholas of Cusa on historical grounds, Lorenzo Valla on philological ones. Both were Catholics; Cusa became a cardinal.</span></div>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-principle">
            <span class="tg-principle__label">What this proves and what it does not</span>
            <p class="tg-principle__text">It does not disprove the doctrine. A true claim can
            be defended with bad evidence, and the modern Catholic case does not rest on
            these documents. What it does is disable one specific argument permanently: no
            appeal to the <em>unbroken continuity</em> of the papal claim can be made without
            accounting for the fact that a substantial portion of the medieval evidence for
            that continuity was manufactured, and that Catholic scholarship is what exposed
            it.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="h-schism">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">1378–1417 · The Great Western Schism</h2>

          <p>This is the sharpest practical problem in the record, and it is not a polemical
          construction — it is the Catholic Church's own history.</p>

          <p>From 1378 there were two claimants to the papacy, each with obedient nations,
          bishops, and canonised saints behind him. From 1409 there were three. For
          thirty-nine years no Christian on earth could say with certainty who the pope
          was, and the question could not be settled by appeal to the papacy, because the
          papacy was the thing in dispute.</p>

          <p>It was resolved by a council. The Council of Constance, in its fifth session,
          declared:</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            …representing the Catholic church militant, it holds power immediately from
            Christ, and that anyone of whatsoever state or dignity, even if it be the papal,
            is bound to obey it in matters which pertain to the faith, the rooting out of the
            said schism, and the general reform of the church in head and members.
            <cite>Council of Constance, <em>Haec Sancta Synodus</em>, session V (6 April 1415)</cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>Constance deposed two claimants, accepted the resignation of a third, and
          elected Martin V. The line of succession Rome recognises as valid today was
          settled by a council acting on the premise that a council outranks a pope — a
          premise later popes rejected, and which Catholic theology has been trying to
          integrate ever since.</p>

          <div class="tg-principle">
            <span class="tg-principle__label">The argument this retires</span>
            <p class="tg-principle__text">The most common practical case for the papacy is
            that it supplies a certainty Protestantism cannot: a living voice that can tell
            you what the church teaches. For thirty-nine years, that voice spoke in triplicate
            and contradicted itself, and the only instrument that could resolve it was the
            one the office denies is superior to it. Whatever the papacy is for, it is not a
            guarantee against exactly this.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="h-vatican">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">1870–today · Vatican I and After</h2>

          <p>Two things are worth knowing about 1870 that rarely make it into popular
          argument on either side.</p>

          <p><strong>The definition was contested inside the council.</strong> A substantial
          minority of bishops — including a large part of the German, Austro-Hungarian,
          French and American episcopate, and historians of the calibre of Hefele — opposed
          the definition as inopportune or as historically unsupportable. Around sixty
          left Rome before the final vote rather than vote against it. The council was
          suspended within weeks when Italian troops entered Rome, so it never took up the
          treatment of the bishops that would have balanced the treatment of the pope.
          Vatican II supplied that ninety-five years later in <em>Lumen Gentium</em> 22–23,
          setting papal primacy inside episcopal collegiality.</p>

          <p><strong>Rome itself now treats the form as open.</strong> In 1995 John Paul II
          did something without real precedent:</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            I am convinced that I have a particular responsibility in this regard, above all in
            acknowledging the ecumenical aspirations of the majority of the Christian
            Communities, and in heeding the request made of me to find a way of exercising the
            primacy which, while in no way renouncing what is essential to its mission, is
            nonetheless open to a new situation.
            <cite>John Paul II, <em>Ut Unum Sint</em> §95 (25 May 1995)</cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>That invitation was taken up. In June 2024 the Dicastery for Promoting Christian
          Unity published <em>The Bishop of Rome: Primacy and Synodality in the Ecumenical
          Dialogues and in the Responses to the Encyclical</em> Ut Unum Sint — a synthesis of
          thirty years of replies from Orthodox, Anglican, Lutheran, Reformed and other
          communions. It discusses, without dismissing them, calls for a "re-reception" of
          Vatican I that would read its definitions in their historical context, and proposes
          a differentiated exercise of the primacy.</p>

          <div class="tg-note">
            <span class="tg-note__label">Why a Protestant should care about that document</span>
            <p>Because it concedes the shape of the problem. The substance is held to be
            unalterable, but the <em>form</em> — how the primacy is exercised, and how much
            of 1870's phrasing was shaped by the nineteenth-century crisis it was written in
            — is treated by Rome's own ecumenical office as open for discussion. Anyone
            arguing that the Catholic position is a monolith that has never examined itself
            should read it first.</p>
          </div>
        </section>
      </section><!-- /part-3 -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           PART IV — RULES OF EVIDENCE
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-part" id="part-4" aria-labelledby="part-4-h">
        <div class="tg-part__head">
          <span class="tg-part__kicker">Part IV</span>
          <h2 class="tg-part__title" id="part-4-h">The Real Crux: Rules of Evidence</h2>
          <p class="tg-part__intro">The two sides largely agree about the facts in Part III.
          What they disagree about is what the facts are allowed to prove. That is why five
          hundred years of argument have not settled it — the rules of evidence are
          themselves the thing in dispute.</p>
        </div>

        <nav class="tg-toc tg-toc--sticky" aria-label="Part IV contents">
          <h2 class="tg-toc__title">In this part</h2>
          <ol class="tg-toc__list">
            <li><a href="#newman">Newman: the Acorn and the Oak</a></li>
            <li><a href="#vincent">Vincent: What the Whole Church Received</a></li>
            <li><a href="#canon">The Canon Objection, Taken Seriously</a></li>
            <li><a href="#orthodox">The Orthodox Witness</a></li>
            <li><a href="#three-questions">Three Questions Each Side Must Answer</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <section class="tg-section" id="newman">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Newman: the Acorn and the Oak</h2>

          <p>John Henry Newman's <em>Essay on the Development of Christian Doctrine</em>
          (1845) is the serious Catholic answer to everything in Part III, and it deserves
          better than the dismissal it usually gets.</p>

          <p>The argument: doctrine develops. The church does not receive a finished system
          of propositions; it receives a living deposit, and comes over time to understand
          explicitly what it always held implicitly. The acorn is not the oak, but the oak
          is what the acorn was <em>for</em>. On this account, the absence of a fully
          articulated papacy in the second century no more disproves it than the absence of
          <em>homoousios</em>, or of a two-natures formula, or of a canon list, disproves
          those. Newman supplied seven notes for distinguishing genuine development from
          corruption — preservation of type, continuity of principles, assimilative power,
          logical sequence, anticipation of its future, conservative action on its past, and
          chronic vigour.</p>

          <p>This is not special pleading. It is a real account of how doctrinal history
          actually looks, and any Protestant who argues "not explicit in the second century,
          therefore false" is holding a rule that would also delete the Nicene formula, the
          doctrine of the two natures, and the canon of the New Testament.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="vincent">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Vincent: What the Whole Church Received</h2>

          <p>The serious answer back is older than the challenge. Vincent of Lérins, writing
          in 434 — a Catholic monk, working out how to tell orthodoxy from novelty:</p>

          <blockquote class="scripture">
            Moreover, in the Catholic Church itself, all possible care must be taken, that we
            hold that faith which has been believed everywhere, always, by all. For that is
            truly and in the strictest sense "Catholic," which, as the name itself and the
            reason of the thing declare, comprehends all universally. This rule we shall
            observe if we follow universality, antiquity, consent.
            <cite>Vincent of Lérins, <em>Commonitory</em> II.6 (NPNF2 XI) · <a href="../../library/read/vincent-of-lerins-commonitory/">read in the Library</a></cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>Apply the three tests to papal supremacy and to Nicene Trinitarianism and they
          come apart:</p>

          <table class="pp-grade">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Test</th><th>Nicene Trinitarianism</th><th>Papal supremacy (claims 6–7)</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Universality</strong></td>
                <td>Confessed by East and West alike, and by every continuing communion since — Catholic, Orthodox, Oriental, Protestant.</td>
                <td>Never received in the East; rejected by half the church; the presenting issue of the schism that split Christendom.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Antiquity</strong></td>
                <td>The material is in the New Testament; the controversy is about how to say it.</td>
                <td>Claims 6 and 7 are not visible in the first three centuries, and the record contains positive counter-evidence.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Consent</strong></td>
                <td>Defined by council and received by the whole church, over decades, after fierce dispute.</td>
                <td>Defined at a council of one communion, in 1870, against sustained internal opposition, with the other communions absent.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <div class="tg-principle">
            <span class="tg-principle__label">The distinction that carries the weight</span>
            <p class="tg-principle__text">Newman is right that doctrine develops. Vincent
            supplies the test for which developments are the church's and which are one
            party's. A development that the whole church ratifies conciliarly looks
            categorically different from one defined <em>against</em> sustained internal
            opposition and never received by the other half. The Trinity passes the test the
            papacy fails, and it fails it on the very ground — catholicity — that the papacy
            exists to secure.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="canon">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Canon Objection, Taken Seriously</h2>

          <p>The strongest card the Catholic side holds is not Matthew 16. It is this:</p>

          <div class="tg-point">
            <span class="tg-point__label">The objection</span>
            <div class="tg-point__heading">You accept a sixty-six book canon on the authority
            of a church whose authority you deny.</div>
            <p>There is no inspired table of contents. Hebrews, James, 2 Peter, 2–3 John,
            Jude and Revelation were disputed for centuries. The list Protestants use for the
            New Testament is the list the fourth-century church settled on — the same church
            with bishops, councils, and a see of Rome. If that church could not be trusted to
            get ecclesiology right, why is it trusted to get the canon right? And if
            Scripture is the sole final norm, what norms the list?</p>
          </div>

          <p>The objection is real, and glib answers are worse than none. Three replies of
          differing strength:</p>

          <ul>
            <li><strong>Recognition is not creation.</strong> The classical Protestant claim
            is that the church <em>received</em> the canon rather than conferring authority
            on it — the books were authoritative as apostolic before any list existed, and
            the councils recorded a consensus that had already largely formed by use. This is
            a real distinction, though it does not eliminate the church's role; it
            characterises it.</li>
            <li><strong>The canon is a different kind of judgment.</strong> Identifying which
            documents are apostolic is a historical question about a closed set with
            observable evidence — authorship, antiquity, catholic use. Defining an office
            with universal immediate jurisdiction is a constitutional claim about the present
            with no comparable evidentiary base. That the church could do the first well
            does not entail it did the second well.</li>
            <li><strong>And the church that settled the canon is not the church that defined
            1870.</strong> The fourth-century consensus was reached by the undivided
            church — East and West, in conciliar process, by the very method Vincent
            describes. The papal definitions were not. A Protestant is not being arbitrary in
            receiving the first and refusing the second; he is applying one consistent
            standard, and it is the standard the ancient church itself used.</li>
          </ul>

          <div class="tg-note">
            <span class="tg-note__label">What honesty requires here</span>
            <p>These replies are good, but they are not costless. A Protestant does depend on
            the judgment of the early church at the point of the canon, and should say so
            without embarrassment rather than pretending to a self-authenticating list. The
            position is not "the church has no authority"; it is "the church's authority is
            real, ministerial, and answerable to the apostolic word it received." Anyone
            arguing this question should be able to say that sentence out loud without
            flinching, because it is what the Reformers actually held. See the companion
            study <a href="../church-authority/">Church Authority, Scripture, and
            Tradition</a> for the longer treatment.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="orthodox">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Orthodox Witness</h2>

          <p>This is the most important consideration in the entire debate, and Protestant
          treatments routinely skip it.</p>

          <p>The Eastern Orthodox churches accept everything Protestants are usually accused
          of rejecting out of modern individualism: apostolic succession, the threefold
          ministry, sacramental theology, the real presence, tradition as authoritative
          alongside Scripture, the seven ecumenical councils, the veneration of saints, the
          Theotokos, monasticism, and a high doctrine of the visible church. They are not
          low-church, not sixteenth-century, and not anti-traditional by any definition.</p>

          <p>And they reject universal jurisdiction and papal infallibility — and always
          have. Their position: Rome held a genuine primacy. First among equals. The senior
          see, whose bishop presided in honour, whose voice carried unique weight, and to
          whom appeal could be made. But not jurisdiction over the other patriarchates, and
          not a charism of infallibility residing in one man apart from the church's
          reception.</p>

          <div class="tg-principle">
            <span class="tg-principle__label">Why this is decisive for how the argument is framed</span>
            <p class="tg-principle__text">It makes it impossible to explain the rejection of
            the papacy as a by-product of the Reformation, of private judgment, of
            nationalism, or of a low view of tradition. A communion that shares Rome's entire
            sacramental and traditional framework, and which was there for the whole first
            millennium, reached the opposite conclusion about claims 6 and 7 — and reached it
            from inside the tradition rather than in reaction against it. The Protestant
            objection is not a novelty; it is the second half of an argument the East was
            already having.</p>
          </div>

          <p>It also supplies a constructive model that neither pure papalism nor pure
          congregationalism offers: a primacy that is real, honoured, and useful — a court of
          appeal, a voice of witness, a focus of unity — without being a jurisdiction. That
          is roughly what the first millennium looks like when read without either
          confession's spectacles, and it is roughly what Rome's own 2024 study document is
          circling.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="three-questions">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Three Questions Each Side Must Answer</h2>

          <table class="pp-grade">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>To the Catholic</th><th>To the Protestant</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>If claims 6–7 were part of the deposit, why did Cyprian and eighty-seven
                bishops, Nicaea's canon 6, Chalcedon's canon 28, and Gregory the Great all
                speak as though they were not — and none of them was corrected for it?</td>
                <td>If the church that gave you the canon was so wrong about its own
                constitution, on what principled basis do you trust its judgment about which
                books are Scripture?</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>If the office guarantees certainty, what was it guaranteeing between 1378
                and 1417 — and by what authority did a council settle it?</td>
                <td>If Scripture is clear, why has <em>sola scriptura</em> produced a
                fragmented and mutually contradicting set of churches, and what is your
                actual mechanism for adjudicating a doctrinal dispute?</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Why does Peter, in the one letter he wrote, describe himself as a fellow
                elder and forbid to church leaders the posture the office later assumed?</td>
                <td>Why do you keep the creeds, the canon, and the Christological
                definitions of councils whose ecclesial authority you deny?</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <p>Neither column is unanswerable. Both columns are uncomfortable. Anyone who finds
          only one of them uncomfortable has not understood the other side.</p>
        </section>
      </section><!-- /part-4 -->

