      <section class="tg-part" id="part-5" aria-labelledby="part-5-h">
        <div class="tg-part__head">
          <span class="tg-part__kicker">Part V</span>
          <h2 class="tg-part__title" id="part-5-h">The Ground to Stand On</h2>
          <p class="tg-part__intro">A verdict on each of the seven claims, the single thread
          that runs through the biblical material, the positive doctrine of church authority
          that a believer can actually live in, and an honest statement of what this
          conclusion depends on and what would overturn it.</p>
          <p class="tg-part__verse"><a class="ref" data-ref="Colossians 1:18">Colossians 1:18</a> · <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 5:1-4">1 Peter 5:1–4</a> · <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 13:17">Hebrews 13:17</a></p>
        </div>

        <nav class="tg-toc tg-toc--sticky" aria-label="Part V contents">
          <h2 class="tg-toc__title">In this part</h2>
          <ol class="tg-toc__list">
            <li><a href="#verdict">The Verdict, Claim by Claim</a></li>
            <li><a href="#thread">The Thread: Christ Holds the Keys</a></li>
            <li><a href="#positive">Five Planks You Can Stand On</a></li>
            <li><a href="#prior">Naming the Prior — and What Would Change It</a></li>
            <li><a href="#charity">How to Hold This Toward Catholic Believers</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <section class="tg-section" id="verdict">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Verdict, Claim by Claim</h2>

          <table class="pp-grade">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>#</th><th>Claim</th><th>Verdict and why</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>1</td>
                <td>Peter's primacy among the Twelve</td>
                <td><strong>Well supported.</strong> First in all four lists, renamed by
                Christ with a commission attached, given the keys, commissioned to shepherd,
                prayed for by name, and dominant in Acts 1–12. Protestants routinely minimise
                this beyond what the text permits.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>2</td>
                <td>Peter died in Rome</td>
                <td><strong>Historically solid.</strong> 1 Clement 5, Ignatius, Dionysius of
                Corinth, Gaius's "trophies," and the natural reading of
                <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 5:13">1 Peter 5:13</a>. Contesting this is a
                waste of a Protestant's credibility.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>3</td>
                <td>Peter as monarchical bishop of Rome</td>
                <td><strong>Weak.</strong> The office did not exist in that form when Peter
                died. 1 Clement uses "bishop" and "presbyter" interchangeably in AD 96;
                Ignatius, whose whole theme is the bishop, has no Roman bishop to name around
                110. Catholic historians largely grant that Rome was led by a college of
                presbyters into the second century.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>4</td>
                <td>Primacy as a transmissible office</td>
                <td><strong>A reasonable inference Scripture never draws.</strong> Isaiah 22
                and Acts 1 make it arguable. But the New Testament's own use of the Isaiah 22
                key gives it to Christ (<a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 3:7">Rev 3:7</a>);
                the qualification for filling an apostolic place is non-transmissible
                (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 1:21-22">Acts 1:21–22</a>); James's death
                produces no replacement; and the letters that organise the post-apostolic
                church install elders and overseers with nothing above them.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>5</td>
                <td>Transmission exclusively to Rome</td>
                <td><strong>Tradition, not Scripture — and the early tradition says something
                milder.</strong> The second- and third-century sources read far more "Rome is
                preeminent" than "Rome alone is Peter's heir." Antioch also claimed Peter;
                Cyprian thought every bishop's chair was Peter's chair.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>6</td>
                <td>Universal, ordinary, immediate jurisdiction</td>
                <td><strong>Counter-evidenced.</strong> Nicaea describes Rome's authority as
                regional and customary; Chalcedon grounds its prerogatives in imperial status;
                Cyprian with eighty-seven bishops denies any bishop of bishops; Victor's
                excommunication failed; Gregory calls the universal title a mark of Antichrist;
                the East never received it.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>7</td>
                <td>Infallibility as defined in 1870</td>
                <td><strong>Counter-evidenced, and the counterexamples bite hardest.</strong>
                Honorius condemned by a council and by his successor; Zosimus reversed;
                Vigilius humiliated; the Schism; Constance. The 1870 conditions are internally
                coherent, but they were formulated afterwards, partly in view of exactly these
                cases — which is what makes them look, historically, like a boundary drawn
                around the damage rather than a description of what the church had always
                believed.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <div class="tg-principle">
            <span class="tg-principle__label">The fair summary</span>
            <p class="tg-principle__text">There is genuine, defensible ground to say the
            papacy as now constituted is a development that goes beyond what Christ
            instituted, and that at specific points it runs against the grain of what
            Scripture teaches about authority in the church. That position is held by the
            Orthodox East, by the Reformation churches, and by a number of Catholic
            historians who then locate their assent in the Church's authority rather than in
            the historical record — which is an intellectually honest place to stand and
            worth respecting.</p>
            <p class="tg-principle__text">But the maximal negative — that the papacy is pure
            invention, a Constantinian corruption, that Rome was nobody until the Middle Ages
            — is not fair to the evidence either, and a believer who argues that way will be
            embarrassed by the first serious Catholic they meet.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="thread">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Thread: Christ Holds the Keys</h2>

          <p>Running underneath every text in Parts I and II there is one line, and it is the
          most right thread available here. It is not primarily a thread <em>against</em>
          Rome. It is a positive account of what the New Testament does with authority.</p>

          <div class="tg-point">
            <span class="tg-point__label">One · The key of David belongs to the King</span>
            <p>Isaiah's steward oracle is picked up exactly once in the New Testament, and
            it lands on Jesus — "who holds the key of David. What He opens no one can shut"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 3:7">Rev 3:7</a>). He holds the keys of Death
            and Hades personally (<a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 1:18">Rev 1:18</a>) —
            which is why the gates of Hades cannot prevail
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 16:18">Matt 16:18</a>). The church's security is
            not a chain of custody. It is whose hand the key is in.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-point">
            <span class="tg-point__label">Two · The keys Peter received were used to open doors, and the doors stayed open</span>
            <p>Watch what Peter actually does with them in Acts. He opens the kingdom to Israel
            at Pentecost (Acts 2), to the Samaritans (Acts 8), and to the Gentiles at
            Cornelius's house (Acts 10) — and he says so himself at the council: "God made a
            choice among you that the Gentiles would hear from my lips the message of the
            gospel and believe" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 15:7">Acts 15:7</a>). That is a
            foundational act, and foundations are laid once. It is precisely the sense in
            which Peter is a rock the church is built on — and precisely why the New Testament
            has no mechanism for a successor to it. The door does not need opening twice.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-point">
            <span class="tg-point__label">Three · What continues is the deposit, carried by ordinary plural office</span>
            <p>The New Testament is intensely interested in continuity — but the thing handed
            on is <em>the word</em>: "the things that you have heard me say among many
            witnesses, entrust these to faithful men who will be qualified to teach others as
            well" (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 2:2">2 Tim 2:2</a>). The carriers are
            elders and overseers, appointed in every town, plural in each congregation,
            qualified by character, and accountable
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 1:5-7">Titus 1:5–7</a>;
            <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 20:17">Acts 20:17</a>, <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 20:28">28</a>;
            <a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 1:1">Phil 1:1</a>). Succession of doctrine,
            yes, emphatically. Succession to Peter's chair, nowhere.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-point">
            <span class="tg-point__label">Four · The one who was given the keys tells us how to hold office</span>
            <p>And this is the hinge. If any man could have claimed monarchy, it was Peter.
            What he wrote is: "As a fellow elder… I appeal to the elders among you… not
            lording it over those entrusted to you, but being examples to the flock. And when
            the Chief Shepherd appears…"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 5:1-4">1 Pet 5:1–4</a>). The rock describes
            himself as one stone among living stones
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 2:5">1 Pet 2:5</a>), the shepherd describes
            himself as an under-shepherd, and the man given the keys tells church leaders not
            to lord it — using the exact verb Christ used of the Gentile kings.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-principle">
            <span class="tg-principle__label">The thread in one sentence</span>
            <p class="tg-principle__text">Christ alone holds the keys and remains the head of
            his church; he gave the apostles a once-laid foundation, with Peter first among
            them to open the doors; and what he left standing afterwards is not a throne but
            a plural, local, accountable, serving eldership carrying an apostolic word that
            outranks all of them — which is why the church is preserved not by the
            indefectibility of one office-holder but by the faithfulness of the Chief Shepherd
            who has not yet appeared.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="positive">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Five Planks You Can Stand On</h2>

          <p>The question was not only "is the papacy true" but "what is the best ground for
          a believer regarding church authority." Those are different questions, and the
          second is more important. Answering the first negatively settles nothing by itself
          — a believer needs somewhere to stand, not merely somewhere to refuse.</p>

          <div class="tg-application">
            <span class="tg-application__label">Plank 1 · Christ is the head, and the position is filled</span>
            <p>"He is the head of the body, the church… so that in all things He may have
            preeminence" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Colossians 1:18">Col 1:18</a>;
            <a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 1:22">Eph 1:22</a>). The church has never for
            one hour been without its head, in any century, under any pope or none. This is
            the first plank because it is the one that removes the anxiety. The visible unity
            of the church does not rest on a man, and its survival never has.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-application">
            <span class="tg-application__label">Plank 2 · Scripture is the final norm — which is not the same as the only authority</span>
            <p>The Reformation claim was <em>norma normans non normata</em>: the norm that
            norms and is not normed. Creeds, councils, confessions, elders and traditions all
            have real authority; they are simply answerable. "Hold to the traditions that you
            were taught by us" (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Thessalonians 2:15">2 Thess
            2:15</a>) and "the church of the living God, the pillar and foundation of the
            truth" (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 3:15">1 Tim 3:15</a>) are in the same
            Bible as the Bereans checking Paul against the Scriptures
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 17:11">Acts 17:11</a>). <em>Sola scriptura</em> was
            never <em>solo scriptura</em> — me and my Bible, with fifteen centuries deleted.
            That distortion is a real error, it has done real damage, and Rome is right to
            press it.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-application">
            <span class="tg-application__label">Plank 3 · Submit to actual, local, plural, accountable authority</span>
            <p>This is where the doctrine becomes practical, and where most Protestants who
            are sound on paper are disobedient in fact. "Obey your leaders and submit to them,
            for they watch over your souls as those who must give an account"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 13:17">Heb 13:17</a>). Not a suggestion. The New
            Testament knows nothing of a Christian with no church, no elders, and no one with
            standing to correct him. If your objection to the papacy is that no man should
            have authority over your conscience, you have not read the New Testament; you have
            read a pamphlet. The biblical pattern is authority that is <em>real</em> — with
            teeth, up to and including exclusion
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 18:17">Matt 18:17</a>) — and simultaneously
            <em>plural, local, qualified by character, and answerable to the word.</em></p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-application">
            <span class="tg-application__label">Plank 4 · Decide together — the conciliar pattern</span>
            <p>Acts 15 is the only decision procedure the New Testament actually shows us:
            the matter is aired, witnesses testify, Scripture is consulted, a judgment is
            framed, and it goes out from "the apostles and elders, with the whole church"
            under the words "It seemed good to the Holy Spirit and to us"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 15:22">Acts 15:22</a>,
            <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 15:28">28</a>). That is why the ecumenical creeds
            carry the weight they do — not because a see decreed them, but because the church
            examined and received them. Hold the creeds. Say them. They are yours.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-application">
            <span class="tg-application__label">Plank 5 · Catholicity is a duty, not a slogan</span>
            <p>"One holy catholic and apostolic church" is in the creed you confess. If the
            objection to Rome is that it broke catholicity by defining against the rest, then
            a Protestant who treats the two thousand years before his own tradition as an
            embarrassment has made the same mistake in the opposite direction. Read the
            Fathers. Learn from the East. Take the historic church as family rather than as
            an opponent's evidence. The strongest version of this position is the one that
            can state the other side's best case without flinching — and the one most at home
            in Christian history, not least at home in it.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="prior">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Naming the Prior — and What Would Change It</h2>

          <p>Here is the thing most treatments of this subject will not say. The verdict above
          is not derived from the evidence alone. It depends on a prior conviction about how
          God preserves his church, and that prior is doing real work.</p>

          <div class="tg-point">
            <span class="tg-point__label">If Scripture is the sole final norm</span>
            <p>…and the apostolic deposit is complete and inscripturated, then silence and
            tension in the record are decisive. An office not visible in the sources for
            three centuries, and contradicted at points by them, has to justify itself —
            and cannot.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-point">
            <span class="tg-point__label">If Christ endowed his church with a living teaching office</span>
            <p>…that carries the deposit forward, then silence is exactly what an acorn
            looks like, development is the norm rather than the problem, and the
            counterexamples are the ordinary friction of history rather than refutations.</p>
          </div>

          <p>Neither side is being irrational. They are applying different rules of evidence,
          and the rules are themselves the thing in dispute. That is not a reason to shrug —
          this study has given a verdict and stands by it — but it is a reason to hold the
          verdict without contempt.</p>

          <div class="tg-note">
            <span class="tg-note__label">What would move this conclusion</span>
            <p>A conclusion that cannot say what would change it is a loyalty, not an
            argument. Three things would substantially move it:</p>
            <p><strong>(a)</strong> Clear second- or third-century evidence, from outside
            Rome, of the church treating a Roman judgment as binding <em>because</em> of the
            Petrine office — not merely deferring to Rome's weight, but acknowledging an
            obligation. Victor's failed excommunication and Cyprian's defiance are currently
            the closest tests, and both went the other way.</p>
            <p><strong>(b)</strong> A demonstration that <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 3:7">Revelation
            3:7</a> and <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 16:19">Matthew 16:19</a> can be shown
            from the text to be complementary in the specific sense the steward reading needs —
            that the New Testament itself understands the key to be delegated onward. At
            present the inference runs the other way.</p>
            <p><strong>(c)</strong> An account of Vincent's canon on which papal supremacy
            passes it. This is the most likely place for the Catholic case to be strengthened,
            and Rome's own 2024 study document is working in that direction.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="tg-section" id="charity">
          <h2 class="tg-section__heading">How to Hold This Toward Catholic Believers</h2>

          <p>Four things, and they matter as much as the argument.</p>

          <div class="tg-principle">
            <span class="tg-principle__label">Get the disagreement in the right place</span>
            <p class="tg-principle__text">The papacy is a serious error about the
            <em>shape</em> of authority in the church. It is not, by itself, a denial of the
            gospel. The Reformation's central material disagreement with Rome was about
            justification, not about ecclesiology — see the companion study
            <a href="../justification/">Justification by Faith</a> — and the ecclesiological
            question is the formal one that keeps the material one unresolved. Getting these
            in the right order prevents a great deal of unnecessary heat.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-principle">
            <span class="tg-principle__label">Assume the person in front of you knows their own faith</span>
            <p class="tg-principle__text">Most Catholic believers are not defending
            <em>Pastor Aeternus</em> chapter 3 from memory; they are worshipping Christ in
            the church that baptised them. An argument that requires them to be stupid or
            dishonest is a bad argument, and it will be obvious to them before it is obvious
            to you.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-principle">
            <span class="tg-principle__label">Concede what is true, immediately and without payment</span>
            <p class="tg-principle__text">Peter's primacy is real. Peter died in Rome. Rome's
            preeminence is early. Rome's doctrinal record in the Christological controversies
            was better than most of its critics'. The <em>petros/petra</em> argument is bad
            and should be dropped. Say all of this first. A person who has heard you give
            ground where ground is due will hear you when you hold it.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-principle">
            <span class="tg-principle__label">Remember what the argument is for</span>
            <p class="tg-principle__text">Christ prayed that they may be one
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 17:21">John 17:21</a>). This question is one of
            the two or three that keep that prayer visibly unanswered, and anyone who enjoys
            arguing it has lost the plot. The right posture toward a division this old is
            grief, clarity, and hope — in that order.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tg-note">
            <span class="tg-note__label">The last word to the other side</span>
            <p>In <em>Ut Unum Sint</em> §95 a pope asked other Christians to help him find a
            new way of exercising the primacy — an admission that the form is open even
            though the substance is held not to be. Whatever one concludes about claims 1
            through 7, that is not the request of a closed system, and it deserves a better
            answer than a slogan.</p>
          </div>
        </section>
      </section><!-- /part-5 -->

