          <span class="pp-src__note">Catholic, readable, and unsparing. The best single
          narrative history.</span></p>

          <p class="pp-src"><cite>The Primacy of Peter: Essays in Ecclesiology and the Early
          Church</cite>, ed. John Meyendorff. Crestwood: St Vladimir's Seminary Press, 1992.
          <span class="pp-src__note">The Orthodox case, including Nicholas Afanasiev's
          influential essay on the eucharistic ecclesiology underlying the Eastern
          objection.</span></p>

          <p class="pp-src"><cite>The Rise of the Papacy</cite>, Robert B. Eno, SS.
          Wilmington: Michael Glazier, 1990.
          <span class="pp-src__note">A Catholic historian's account of the development,
          notably measured on the second and third centuries.</span></p>

          <p class="pp-src"><cite>Upon This Rock: St. Peter and the Primacy of Rome in
          Scripture and the Early Church</cite>, Stephen K. Ray. San Francisco: Ignatius,
          1999.
          <span class="pp-src__note">The Catholic apologetic case, densely footnoted. Read it
          for the strongest popular-level statement of claims 1–5.</span></p>

          <p class="pp-src"><cite>The Church of Rome at the Bar of History</cite>, William
          Webster. Edinburgh: Banner of Truth, 1995.
          <span class="pp-src__note">The Protestant historical case, and the standard
          collection of the patristic counter-evidence.</span></p>

          <p class="pp-src"><cite>An Essay on the Development of Christian Doctrine</cite>,
          John Henry Newman, 1845 (rev. 1878).
          <span class="pp-src__note">Not optional. Any judgment against the papacy that has
          not reckoned with Newman is provisional. Newman's <em>Apologia Pro Vita Sua</em>
          is in this site's <a href="../../library/">Library</a>.</span></p>

          <p class="pp-src"><cite>The Gospel According to Matthew</cite>, D. A. Carson
          (Expositor's Bible Commentary), on 16:13–20.
          <span class="pp-src__note">Cited in Part I for the judgment that the
          <em>petros/petra</em> argument does not carry the weight commonly placed on it —
          from a commentator who does not draw Catholic conclusions from that
          concession.</span></p>
        </section>

