        <div class="tg-reading">
          <h2 class="tg-reading__title">If You Read Four Things</h2>
          <p class="tg-reading__item">
            <cite>Papal Primacy: From Its Origins to the Present</cite>, Klaus Schatz.
            <em>The most useful single book on this page. A Catholic historian who will tell
            you what the sources say before telling you what he concludes from them.</em>
          </p>
          <p class="tg-reading__item">
            <cite>An Essay on the Development of Christian Doctrine</cite>, John Henry Newman.
            <em>The argument you have to beat. If your position cannot survive Newman, it is
            not a position yet.</em>
          </p>
          <p class="tg-reading__item">
            <cite>The Primacy of Peter</cite>, ed. John Meyendorff.
            <em>The Orthodox voice — which is the one that shows this is not a Protestant
            quarrel.</em>
          </p>
          <p class="tg-reading__item">
            <cite>The Bishop of Rome</cite> (Dicastery for Promoting Christian Unity, 2024).
            <em>Free, current, and the clearest evidence that Rome regards the form of the
            primacy as an open question.</em>
          </p>
        </div>
